======================================================================================================================
CHECKS
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] 119a (a WORKS, and it is the item's zero-point test) with a_0 FIXED by the cosmological constant and nothing else free, solving each SPARC rotation curve for its own stellar mass-to-light ratio returns a population that sits on the stellar-population value: the derived Upsilon at the sample's median colour is within 0.1 dex of 0.5 on BOTH footings   (canonical Upsilon(median colour) = 0.528 +- 0.039 (+0.023 dex from SPS 0.50 +- 0.10); alt 0.476 +- 0.032 (-0.022 dex); medians 0.505 / 0.456 over N = 70 / 69)
  [PASS] 119b the SLOPE of the derived relation is shallow, which is what the near-infrared stellar-population calibrations say and is the OPPOSITE of the steep optical colour-M/L relations -- and this is the footing-independent half of the measurement, so a_0's own factor-1.2 ambiguity cannot move it   (d log Upsilon_[3.6] / d(B-V)_oT = +0.143 +- 0.244 (canonical), +0.181 +- 0.243 (alt); near-IR bracket (0.0, 0.3), optical-band bracket (0.6, 1.8); with the observed colour, +0.088 +- 0.206.  AGAINST INTEREST, the CENTRAL VALUE is estimator-dependent: weighting by the measurement budget moves it to -0.001 +- 0.243, i.e. to zero.  What is robust is the BRACKET -- every version lands inside the near-infrared one -- not the coincidence with any particular published coefficient, and this item must not be quoted as reproducing 0.135)
  [PASS] 119c AGAINST INTEREST -- the POWER, stated so the pass above is not over-read: the steep optical-like slope is only 1.9 sigma away, so this measurement CONFIRMS the shallow near-infrared calibration without EXCLUDING the steep one at 3 sigma.  The relation is derived, but it is not yet a discriminating measurement of the colour term   (slope +0.143 +- 0.244; the shallow near-IR value sits at -0.0 sigma, the low edge of the optical bracket at +1.9 sigma; 3 sigma would need sigma(slope) < 0.152, i.e. 179 galaxies at this scatter, or the same 70 with the distance budget removed)
  [PASS] 119d the SCATTER is consistent with stellar populations too -- but this is a BOUND, not a measurement, and the check text is written to say so.  The observed 0.25 dex scatter about the derived relation is almost entirely accounted for by the distance and inclination budgets alone; what is left is 0.12 dex against the 0.10 dex stellar populations allow at 3.6 um.  Since the budget nearly saturates the observed scatter, this sample has essentially NO power to detect an intrinsic Upsilon spread, and the agreement is a non-detection rather than a confirmation   (scatter about the derived relation 0.253 dex; measurement budget (stat 0.018, distance 0.138, inclination 0.044) = 0.225 dex rms; implied intrinsic 0.116 dex against the SPS allowance of 0.10.  A 10% error in the budget moves the intrinsic figure between 0.053 and 0.152 dex, which is the whole allowed range -- that is what 'no power' looks like numerically.  And the median reduced chi^2 of the fits is 2.55 (mean 4.99), so some of the residual is model error -- non-circular motions and radial M/L gradients a single Upsilon per galaxy cannot absorb -- which pushes the true intrinsic M/L scatter DOWN, not up.  The distance budget is the thing to remove: TRGB/Cepheid distances would)
        cut the dominant 0.138 dex line and turn this bound into a measurement, which is exactly what item 103 asks for.
  [PASS] 119e the colour trend is not luminosity or inclination in disguise: controlling for log L[3.6] leaves the colour coefficient where it was, the luminosity coefficient is consistent with zero, and the residual does not correlate with inclination -- which is the trap the extinction-corrected colour could have set, since its internal-extinction term is itself a function of inclination   (joint fit: colour +0.218 +- 0.166 (alone +0.143), log L[3.6] -0.017 +- 0.049 (-0.3 sigma); residual-vs-inclination r = -0.019 over N = 70)

  Hubble-type proxy on the larger sample (the fallback the item allows): N = 120, d log Upsilon/dT = -0.0058 +- 0.0107 dex per type unit, scatter 0.274 dex
  the overlap measures d(B-V)_oT/dT = -0.0400 mag per type unit, so the type slope maps onto a colour slope of +0.144 +- 0.268 -- consistent with the direct colour fit, on a 1.7x larger sample
  [PASS] 119f the Hubble-type version, on the 1.7x larger sample that needs no colour cross-match, gives the same answer: a shallow trend of the derived Upsilon with type, which converts through the measured d(B-V)/dT into a colour slope consistent with the direct one and still inside the near-infrared bracket   (type slope -0.0058 +- 0.0107/type -> colour slope +0.144 +- 0.268 vs the direct +0.143 +- 0.244)

  item 76's deep-tail estimator on the same data requires Upsilon = 0.656 (canonical) / 0.504 (alt); this item's full-curve per-galaxy fit gives medians 0.505 / 0.456
  [PASS] 119g AGAINST INTEREST -- an estimator-choice systematic on the headline number of item 76, found here and not there.  The deep-tail intercept and the full-curve per-galaxy fit are two ways of reading the SAME rotation curves at the SAME fixed a_0, and they disagree by about 0.1 dex in Upsilon.  Item 76's '0.656 canonical / 0.504 alt' therefore carries an estimator systematic of that size which was not quoted, and the 'mildly prefers the alt footing' reading is inside it   (deep-tail 0.656 vs full-curve median 0.505 = 0.113 dex (canonical); 0.504 vs 0.456 = 0.043 dex (alt).  The two footings are 0.082 dex apart, i.e. comparable to the estimator systematic itself)

======================================================================================================================
MUTATION CONTROLS
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M119-1 the colour-shuffle null, and it says the trend is NOT detected.  Randomly reassigning the colours produces a slope as large as the measured one 55% of the time.  That is the honest reading of a shallow relation and it is reported as such: what this item establishes is the ZERO-POINT of the derived Upsilon and a BOUND on its colour term, not a detection of a colour trend   (shuffled slopes: mean -0.008, spread 0.215; the measured +0.143 is +0.7 sigma from the null and is matched or exceeded in 52% of shuffles)
  [PASS] M119-2 the ZERO-POINT can fail and does: tripling a_0 moves the derived Upsilon by the -1 power the algebra demands and takes it far outside the stellar-population range, so check 119a is a real test of the value of a_0 and not a tautology of the fitting procedure   (a_0 x 3 -> median Upsilon 0.214 (-0.37 dex from SPS), against 0.505 at the true value; measured d log U/d log a_0 = -0.78 (algebra says -1 in the deep-MOND limit))
  [PASS] M119-3 MY OWN CLAIM, CORRECTED BY ITS OWN CONTROL.  This check was first written as 'the slope survives a factor-3 change in a_0 untouched', and it FAILED: the slope moves from +0.14 to +0.72.  The reason is real and is now stated instead of hidden -- SPARC is not entirely in the deep-MOND limit, redder galaxies sit at higher g_bar/a_0 and so respond LESS to a change in a_0, and a wrong a_0 therefore TILTS the relation as well as sliding it.  What is actually true, and is what the item needs, is the narrow statement: over the range the two footings span the tilt is a sixth of the slope's own error.  The slope is footing-insensitive; it is NOT a_0-free in general, and a large a_0 error would masquerade as a colour term   (across the actual footing range (0.082 dex in a_0) the slope moves 0.038, against its bootstrap error 0.244; across a factor of 3 (0.477 dex) it moves 0.576, i.e. more than the slope itself (+0.720 vs +0.143).  Implied sensitivity d(slope)/d log a_0 = 0.46 locally, 1.21 over the wide range -- non-linear, as the kernel's curvature requires)
  [PASS] M119-4 the kernel is load-bearing.  With nu = 1 -- pure Newton, no dark matter, the same curves, the same fits -- the mass-to-light ratio the discs would need is nearly three times the stellar-population value and the fits are five times worse.  So the Upsilon that lands on stellar populations in check 119a comes from the kernel and the value of a_0, not from the rotation curves alone   (Newtonian fits: median Upsilon 1.43 = +0.46 dex from SPS (64% of galaxies above 2x SPS), median reduced chi^2 14.4 against 2.55 with the kernel; and the Newtonian Upsilon has no reason to sit anywhere in particular, which is the point -- its spread is 0.27 dex against 0.25 with the kernel)

======================================================================================================================
THE LambdaCDM / NEWTONIAN ALTERNATIVE, COMPUTED BESIDE
======================================================================================================================
  With a dark halo the rotation curve does NOT determine Upsilon at all -- the halo absorbs any value below the maximum-disc bound.  That bound, computed here inside 2.2 disc scale lengths, has median 0.66 and spans 0.19 - 2.30: it is an UPPER LIMIT per galaxy, not a value, and 67% of the sample has it above the stellar-population 0.5, i.e. no information.
  So the LambdaCDM side of this item has no prediction to compare: a Upsilon(colour) relation cannot be derived from rotation curves in a theory with a free halo.  The framework's derivation exists only because a_0 is fixed by Lambda and the kernel leaves no other freedom.  That is the asymmetry the item is testing, and it is real -- but it also means this item cannot DISCRIMINATE between the theories, only demonstrate that one of them makes the prediction.

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================================
  The relation exists and it is derived, not fitted: a_0 = c/2 sqrt(G rho_DE) fixed, one parameter per galaxy, and the
  answer lands on stellar populations.  Zero-point Upsilon(median colour) = 0.528 (canonical) / 0.476 (alt)
  against SPS 0.50 +- 0.10; slope d log Upsilon/d(B-V)_oT = +0.143 +- 0.244, inside the near-infrared
  bracket and footing-insensitive.  Four things are recorded AGAINST it.  (1) The colour trend is NOT detected: a
  shuffle of the colours reproduces it in 52% of trials, so what stands is the zero-point and a BOUND on the colour
  term, and the steep optical-band value is only 1.9 sigma away.  (2) The per-galaxy scatter agrees with stellar
  populations (0.12 dex intrinsic against 0.10) only because the distance and inclination budget already saturates
  the observed 0.25 dex -- a non-detection, not a confirmation.  (3) The slope's a_0-independence is LOCAL, not exact:
  a factor-3 error in a_0 tilts it by 0.58, more than the slope itself, because SPARC is not entirely deep-MOND.
  (4) The same curves read with item 76's deep-tail estimator give a Upsilon 0.11 dex away -- an estimator systematic
  comparable to the whole gap between the two footings, which item 76 did not quote and which this item found.

